"use strict";(self.webpackChunkmy_website=self.webpackChunkmy_website||[]).push([[894],{6042:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"blogPosts":[{"id":"how-this-site-is-deployed","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/how-this-site-is-deployed","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-16-how-this-site-is-deployed.mdx","title":"How this website is built, tested, and deployed","description":"The libraries, local checks, GitHub Actions workflow, and GitHub Pages hosting behind radel.dev.","date":"2026-11-16T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"November 16, 2026","tags":[{"label":"Docusaurus","permalink":"/blog/tags/docusaurus"},{"label":"CI/CD","permalink":"/blog/tags/ci-cd"},{"label":"GitHub Pages","permalink":"/blog/tags/git-hub-pages"}],"readingTime":1.355,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Sean Radel","title":"Senior IT Systems Engineer","url":"https://github.com/seanradel2020","imageURL":"/img/sean-portrait-2026.jpg","key":"sean"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"how-this-site-is-deployed","title":"How this website is built, tested, and deployed","authors":["sean"],"tags":["Docusaurus","CI/CD","GitHub Pages"],"description":"The libraries, local checks, GitHub Actions workflow, and GitHub Pages hosting behind radel.dev."},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Evaluating Privacy Questions from Stack Overflow: Can ChatGPT Compete?","permalink":"/blog/chatgpt-stackoverflow-privacy"}},"content":"import DeploymentDiagram from \'@site/src/components/DeploymentDiagram\';\\n\\nThis website is a static Docusaurus site, built with GitHub Actions and hosted on GitHub Pages at **[radel.dev](https://radel.dev)**.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<DeploymentDiagram />\\n\\n## Libraries & content\\n\\n- **Docusaurus 3 + React 18:** routing, documentation, blog pages, and reusable components.\\n- **Markdown + MDX:** posts and documentation; **CSS Modules + Infima** provide the styling, and **Prism** highlights code.\\n- **React + SVG:** the landing-page impact diagram and interactive travel map. Country outlines come from local Natural Earth data, without a map API.\\n- **Static output:** HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and images in `build/`. No application server or database.\\n\\n## Testing before a merge\\n\\n- **Map tests:** `node --test src/components/TravelMap/mapUtils.test.mjs` checks destinations, coordinates, pan/zoom limits, and regional views.\\n- **Production build:** `npm run build` compiles the site and rejects broken internal URLs.\\n- **Browser checks:** review desktop, tablet, and phone layouts, navigation, images, and map interactions with `npm run serve`.\\n- **CI coverage:** the deployment workflow runs the production build; the map tests and browser checks are currently local pre-merge checks.\\n\\n## Deployment & hosting\\n\\n- **Trigger:** every push to `main`, including a merged pull request, starts the **Deploy to GitHub Pages** workflow.\\n- **Runner:** Ubuntu checks out the repository with `actions/checkout@v4` and installs Node.js 18 with `actions/setup-node@v4` and npm caching.\\n- **Build:** `npm ci --force` installs the locked dependencies; `npm run build` generates `build/`.\\n- **Publish:** `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3` uses `GITHUB_TOKEN` with `contents: write` to publish `build/` to the `gh-pages` branch.\\n- **Domain:** the publish action writes `CNAME` for `radel.dev`; GitHub Pages serves the branch root over HTTPS, with the domain\u2019s DNS pointing to Pages.\\n\\n[Source code](https://github.com/seanradel2020/website) \xb7 [Deployment workflow](https://github.com/seanradel2020/website/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml)"},{"id":"chatgpt-stackoverflow-privacy","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/chatgpt-stackoverflow-privacy","source":"@site/blog/2023-9-04-chatgpt-stackoverflow.md","title":"Evaluating Privacy Questions from Stack Overflow: Can ChatGPT Compete?","description":"An overview of our 2023 IEEE paper comparing ChatGPT responses with Stack Overflow answers to developers\' privacy questions.","date":"2023-09-04T00:00:00.000Z","formattedDate":"September 4, 2023","tags":[{"label":"AI","permalink":"/blog/tags/ai"},{"label":"Privacy","permalink":"/blog/tags/privacy"},{"label":"Research","permalink":"/blog/tags/research"}],"readingTime":2.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Sean Radel","title":"Senior IT Systems Engineer","url":"https://github.com/seanradel2020","imageURL":"/img/sean-portrait-2026.jpg","key":"sean"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"chatgpt-stackoverflow-privacy","title":"Evaluating Privacy Questions from Stack Overflow: Can ChatGPT Compete?","authors":["sean"],"tags":["AI","Privacy","Research"],"description":"An overview of our 2023 IEEE paper comparing ChatGPT responses with Stack Overflow answers to developers\' privacy questions."},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How this website is built, tested, and deployed","permalink":"/blog/how-this-site-is-deployed"}},"content":"In 2023, I co-authored an IEEE paper with the Privacy Engineering Regulatory Compliance Lab exploring how ChatGPT answers developers\' privacy questions compared with Stack Overflow. This post brings together the abstract and an overview of that work; the findings reflect the models evaluated in the original study.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n### Abstract\\n\\nStack Overflow and other similar forums are commonly used by developers to seek answers for their software development as well as privacy-related concerns. Recently, ChatGPT has been used as an alternative to generate code or produce responses to developers\' questions. In this paper, we aim to understand developers\' privacy challenges by evaluating the types of privacy-related questions asked on Stack Overflow. We then conduct a comparative analysis between the accepted responses given by Stack Overflow users and the responses produced by ChatGPT for those extracted questions to identify if ChatGPT could serve as a viable alternative. Our results show that most privacy-related questions are related to choice/consent, aggregation, and identification. Furthermore, our findings illustrate that ChatGPT generates similarly correct responses for about 56% of questions while for the rest of the responses, the answers from Stack Overflow are slightly more accurate than ChatGPT. \\n\\n**[Click Here for More Information](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10260753), [Click Here to Read for Free](https://arxiv.org/ftp/arxiv/papers/2306/2306.11174.pdf)**\\n\\n### About\\n**This paper was written with members of the [PERC_Lab](https://www.sepidehghanavati.com/research). Zack Delile led the project, and I was a co-author alongside Joe Godinez, Garrett Engstrom, Theo Brucker, Kenzie Young, and Sepideh Ghanavati.**  \\n\\nIn our research, we sought to assess whether ChatGPT could serve as a viable alternative to Stack Overflow in answering privacy-related questions and generating similarly correct results. Our research was based on 932 questions extracted between 2016 and 2023 from Stack Overflow by lab memeber Sam Morse. Next, we randomly selected 270 questions and answers from the data set,then we conducted a comparative analysis with the responses received from ChatGPT. Next, we developed an annotation strategy to classify the types of QAs based on two well-known privacy taxonomies. After four rounds of annotations with three groups of two annotators (i.e., six in total), and two rounds of discussions to resolve the differences, we create a dataset of 92 pairs of multi-labeled privacy-related QAs. \\n\\n\\nWe then follow prompt engineering and generate\\nanswers for Stack Overflow privacy-related questions in ChatGPT and develop a study to identify whether the solutions\\nfrom ChatGPT match with the accepted answers or the highest\\nupvoted answers from Stack Overflow.\\nOur results show that when given the same prompt, ChatGPT responses match with Stack Overflow answers in 56.1% of cases. When it does not match, SO is slightly more accurate than ChatGPT. This result indicates that ChatGPT may in the future be used as an alternative to SO and other forums. However, since both of these tools show an accuracy of less than 75%, developers need to use the forums and ChatGPT with caution to ensure compliance with regulations and protect users\u2019 privacy."}]}')}}]);